Finding: {"title":"generate-stage endpoint checks org membership but not build ownership — any org user can drive another user's incremental build","severity":"MEDIUM","area":"Authorization","files":["src/server/routes/routes-ai-curriculum-incremental.ts"],"detail":"POST /ai-curriculum/incremental/:buildId/generate-stage (lines 556-664), GET /ai-curriculum/incremental/:buildId (lines 465-488), and POST .../approve-outline and .../approve-stage all filter the build lookup only by `eq(curriculumIncrementalBuilds.id, buildId)` + `eq(..., flightSchoolId, orgId)` — they never check `eq(..., userId, authUser.id)`. Only the DELETE endpoint (lines 813-834) additionally scopes by `userId`. Since builds are per-user, drafty, in-progress state (start.ts explicitly cancels 'any existing in-progress builds for this location+user' to avoid orphans, implying builds are meant to be user-scoped), any authenticated user within the same flight school/org can view, drive stage generation for, approve stages into, and publish another user's in-progress incremental curriculum build by guessing/enumerating its UUID buildId.","impact":"Cross-user interference within the same org: one instructor/admin could inadvertently (or via a leaked/logged buildId) advance, approve-into, or publish another user's WIP curriculum draft, consuming their AI budget and potentially publishing content the original author never reviewed.","recommendation":"Add `eq(curriculumIncrementalBuilds.userId, authUser.id)` to the WHERE clause on all incremental build routes that currently only check orgId, consistent with the DELETE route's existing pattern — or make an explicit product decision that these are org-shared drafts and document it.","confidence":"medium"}
